5:[["$","$L10","1.2",{"topic":{"id":"1.2","title":"Concept and Evolution of Cyber Law","minutes":12,"summary":"From legal recognition of electronic records to platform accountability and synthetic media: trace why the legal framework keeps changing.","sections":[{"title":"From paper equivalence to digital regulation","body":["The UNCITRAL Model Law on Electronic Commerce, 1996 influenced the principle that information should not lose legal recognition merely because it is electronic. India’s Information Technology Act, 2000 introduced recognition for electronic records, authentication mechanisms, offences, and institutional powers. It also amended existing laws so that paper-based concepts could accommodate electronic processes, with specific exclusions that must be checked in the statute.","The 2008 amendment, brought into force in 2009, replaced the original section 66 and expanded the framework with provisions including sections 66A–66F, 67A–67C, and 69A. It introduced section 43A on reasonable security practices for specified body-corporate failures and section 79A for expert examiners. These provisions have different purposes and must not be treated as interchangeable."]},{"title":"Institutions, privacy, and platform duties","body":["The 2011 SPDI Rules addressed specified sensitive personal data handled by body corporates. The 2015 Shreya Singhal judgment struck down section 66A and read down “actual knowledge” in the intermediary context. The 2017 Puttaswamy judgment recognized privacy as a fundamental right. The 2021 Intermediary Guidelines and Digital Media Ethics Code established due-diligence and grievance mechanisms, later amended for areas including online gaming and synthetic information.","The three new criminal laws commenced on 1 July 2024, replacing the IPC, CrPC, and Evidence Act subject to savings. The Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 is a separate framework; enactment alone does not establish that every provision is operationally in force. A current answer should identify the version of the law, its commencement date, and its relationship to other regimes."]},{"title":"Change is not automatic replacement of every rule","body":["A later notification may amend particular words in an earlier rule rather than replace the entire instrument. For example, the February 2026 amendment changed the 2021 Rules and added synthetic-media duties. The 2011 Rules are not simply “the 2021 Rules with a different year”. Likewise, repeal of the IPC does not erase earlier liabilities or pending proceedings.","For exam and practice answers, draw a timeline: enactment, notification, commencement, amendment, judicial interpretation. Use the official consolidated text where available, then inspect the amending notification and corrigenda. A news article or old textbook may accurately describe the past while being unreliable as a current statement of law."]}],"practice":{"title":"Build a legal timeline","scenario":"A textbook written in 2018 discusses section 66A and a section 65B certificate.","steps":["Place the IT Act, its 2008 amendment, Shreya Singhal, and the new criminal laws on a timeline.","Mark section 66A as struck down rather than merely amended.","Explain why a 2018 discussion of section 65B is historically useful.","Identify the current BSA provision and its Schedule without treating old judgments as irrelevant."],"debrief":"Legal evolution is cumulative. Current procedure may change while older judgments still explain principles, subject to the new statutory text and later authority."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["it-act","it-2021","bsa"],"sources":["itact","shreya","privacy","bsa","it2026"],"labId":"$undefined","unit":1,"objectives":["Explain from paper equivalence to digital regulation and apply it to an investigation.","Explain institutions, privacy, and platform duties and apply it to an investigation.","Explain change is not automatic replacement of every rule and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-1.2","prompt":"When did the BNS, BNSS and BSA generally commence?","options":["26 January 2023","1 July 2024","1 January 2021","20 February 2026"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The three new criminal laws generally commenced on 1 July 2024, with savings and transitional rules.","unit":1}}}]]
